# SYNOPSIS

```text
tcp!*!17019
/lib/cpu/terminal
```

# DESCRIPTION

The cpu server runs rc(1) for a terminal on another machine, as Plan 9's cpu server does: rc on this node, its terminal the caller's, with the caller's files at `/mnt/term`.
The caller is vx-cpu(1) on the dev machine.

It is only in an image built with `vx boot --cpu` (vx-build(1)), which puts the terminal's public key, 64 hex digits, at bootfs's `lib/cpu/terminal`; no other image runs it, and nothing else listens on its port.
It announces `tcp!*!17019` through its namespace's `/net` (ip(4)) and prints `cpu: listening on tcp!*!17019, key` and this node's public key, the key a terminal names to reach it.

A call is taken only from the link: its address must fall in a route of `/net/iproute` that reaches it without a router (type `4i` or `6i`), so a call that came through a gateway is refused.
The call is then sealed by Noise IK (noise(2)) with the prologue `cpu` and empty payloads, the caller the initiator, through factotum's `proto=noise-ik` key (factotum(4)), the same key 9gate(4) uses: cpu's manifest grants it that and nothing else.
A caller whose key isn't the pinned terminal's gets no reply.

The terminal's first message is the command record, `size[4] command[size]` (little-endian, at most 8192 bytes): the command for `rc -c`, or empty for an interactive rc (`rc -i`).
The connection then carries 9P2000 (9p(5)) the other way: the terminal serves its tree and the server imports it (gate(2)), as Plan 9's cpu does with exportfs.

rc starts in a namespace of its own: the server's, with the terminal's tree at `/mnt/term`, its `dev/cons` bound over `/dev/cons`, and a new environment group at `/env`.
Its namespace has fs(4)'s volume at `/data`, where files copied from `/mnt/term` land, `/dev/bin`, where a program copied runs in place of bootfs's when its service restarts (launcher(8)), and the launcher's tree at `/svc/launcher`.
It has no descriptors, so it takes `/dev/cons` as its terminal (shell(8)).
It runs in a job of its own, its note group, under the server's.
Each note the terminal sends, read from `/mnt/term/dev/cpunote`, is written to `/proc/`<pid>`/notepg` for rc's pid (proc(4)), so ^C on the terminal interrupts what rc is running.
If the terminal goes, rc's group is sent `hangup`.
When rc ends, whatever it left running in its job is killed, the server prints how it ended, and it hangs up.

Each session is a thread of its own; sessions run at the same time.

Its manifest:

```text
service cpu
program cpu
programs
use env
use factotum
grant factotum proto=noise-ik
mount /bin bootfs/bin
mount /lib/man bootfs/lib/man
mount /lib/cpu bootfs/lib/cpu
mount /dev devmgr/dev
mount /net devmgr/net
mount /net net/net after
mount /net cs/net after
mount /net dns/net after
mount /proc proc
mount /mnt/plumb plumber
mount /data fs/volume create
mount /dev/bin fs/volume/bin create
launcher
restart on-failure
```

# EXAMPLES

From the dev machine, in the tree, run a new build of the plumber without a reboot:

```rc
% vx cpu -k $nodekey -r build/amd64/kernel -c \
    'cp /mnt/term/root/plumber.elf /dev/bin/plumber &&
     echo restart plumber >/svc/launcher/ctl' node
```

